Blend Graphics primitives drawn with a translucent color.

Graphics ignored the alpha of the color that a Nokia game sets through
DirectGraphics.setARGBColor(), so translucent panels covered the picture
below them. Graphics now keeps the alpha of its current color, 255 being
opaque; the setColor() methods and a reset of the context restore 255.

A primitive with a color below 255 is blended once over the pixels that it
covers, clipped and translated like an opaque one. The primitive is drawn in
black on a white image of its bounding box. Gray pixels in the mask carry
the fractional glyph coverage. The pixels below are read back, composited
using the product of coverage and color alpha, and written without
further alpha processing, because drawRGB() weakens the alpha on RGB565
targets. Alpha 0 draws nothing. Opaque primitives still call the natives
directly.

The natives that draw the lines, rectangles, rounded rectangles, arcs,
triangles and text become private methods with a trailing 0 so that the public
methods can choose between them.

